        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "For each prime, the HCF uses the smaller index and the LCM the larger. Their sum equals the sum of the two original indices, so the products are equal.",
          "worked": "For any prime $p$, let its indices in the two numbers be $r$ and $s$, using index zero if the prime is absent. Multiplication adds these indices, giving $p^{r+s}$ in the product of the numbers.\n\nThe HCF uses the smaller of $r$ and $s$, and the LCM uses the larger. Multiplying the HCF and LCM adds those indices. The smaller plus the larger is $r+s$, so each prime has the same index in both products. Therefore the HCF multiplied by the LCM equals the product of the two numbers.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 35
      }
    ]
